# Line endings for web/, which `make sync-<game>` in the website repo copies
# into arcade/ verbatim.
#
# Deliberately no eol pin here, matching the website repo, which leaves html,
# css and js to each clone's core.autocrlf. The sync is a byte copy, so the two
# repos have to agree: pinning web/ to LF while the website checks arcade/ out
# as CRLF leaves that repo full of files git reports as modified even though
# `git diff` shows nothing -- normalisation hides it, so the content is
# identical and only the bytes on disk differ. Noise, not damage, but it buries
# the one file that did change. Agreeing costs nothing: both repos normalise to
# LF in the index, so what is committed is LF either way.
#
# Pinned below are the files CRLF breaks outright on Linux rather than merely
# making untidy: a trailing \r on a shebang gives "bad interpreter", and a
# workflow's `run:` block is handed to a Linux shell verbatim.
*.sh        text eol=lf
*.yml       text eol=lf
*.yaml      text eol=lf

# tui/ is Python, and unlike web/ it is not byte-copied anywhere, so nothing
# has to agree with it. Pin it: core.autocrlf is true on the machines this is
# written on and does NOT normalise on the way in, so a working copy that has
# drifted to CRLF stages a whole-file rewrite -- 236 insertions on a file where
# five lines changed -- which buries the real edit. Committed content is LF
# either way; this keeps the checkout LF too, so the drift never starts.
*.py        text eol=lf
*.toml      text eol=lf
*.mjs       text eol=lf
